ps://www.discogs.com/label/1403595-Musicrama","type":"label","urls":[]}},"/api/discogs/label/1610/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1610","image":"https://i.discogs.com/aNZfcLSVMW8xdCMyTxY3PspGfkoD4lsz8_uyffeyRcw/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTE2MTAt/MTU4ODY3Mzk0Ny0x/NjI2LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/WBUlZyXAUxSK9LL1iYZuLgPkqmFs2ybCdpW-P6beKk8/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTE2MTAt/MTU4ODY3Mzk0Ny0x/NjI2LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Multinational record label established in 1913, acquired by the [l=Universal Music Group] in December 1998 as part of the acquisition of the [l=Polygram] group, effective January 1999.\n\nLabel Code: LC 0309 / LC 00309.\n\n[b]Please note:[/b] PolyGram seven-numeral catalogue numbers were introduced in April, 1970 - any previous release with such a number is a reissue from after that date.\nThis catalogue numbering system was replaced with a new system initiated in August 1982 and tied to the barcode/EAN. It had six digits followed by a single number suffix denoting the format, usually xxxxxx-x. In the US, when it was introduced in February 1983, this was often prefixed by a three-digit code denoting a company division, i.e. xxx xxxxxx-x, and sometimes a further one-letter suffix (for example 422 822104-1Y). These new numbers were not seen outside Japan until early 1983 when compact discs were first released to the public outside that country.\nRim text on labels of German LPs \"Urheber- Und ...\" was replaced by \"Alle Urheber- Und ...\" late 1976 / early 1977. \n\nWords such as Deluxe, Super, Special and Select are not part of the catalogue number - these are price codes and can be entered as identifiers.\n\nGerman Polydor post-war price codes for formats and series numbers (1950s and 1960s):\nEPH: 45 rpm, 17 cm, EP, Mono, 20 000 and 21 000\nNH: 45 rpm, 17 cm, Mono,  22 000 to 24 000 and 66 000\nKN: 45 rpm, 17 cm, Mono, 55 000\nSEPH: 45 rpm, 17 cm, EP, Stereo, 224 000\nSNH: 45 rpm, 17 cm, Stereo, 220 000\nSKN: 45 rpm, 17 cm, Stereo, 223 000\nLPH: 33 rpm, 25 cm, Mono, 45 000\nLPHM: 33 rpm, 30 cm, Mono, 46 000\nHPM: 33 rpm, 30 cm, Mono, 47 000\nSLPH: 33 rpm, 25 cm, Stereo, 235 000\nSLPHM: 33 rpm, 30 cm, Stereo, 237 000\n\nGerman label rim texts:\n1950 - September 1952: \"Alle Rechte...\" at 12 o'clock.\nSeptember 1952 - December 1963: \"Alle Hersteller...\" at 7 o'clock.\nDecember 1959 - December 1963: \"- Alle Hersteller...\" at 1 o'clock.\nNovember 1963 - December 1968: \"Made in Germany by...\" at 2 o'clock.\nJanuary 1969 - April 1972: \"Urheber-... \" at 10 o'clock, counter-clockwise (delayed releases also May 1972).\nApril 1972 - December 1976: \"Urheber-...\" at 2 o'clock, clockwise.\nSince January 1977: \"Alle Urheber...\"\n\nFor all unofficial releases (bootlegs, counterfeits, pirate compilations, etc.) that pretend to be Polydor releases, please use [L=Polydor (2)].\n\n[b]Timeline[/b]\n2 April 1913: Establishment of Polydor as a brand by German [l=Polyphonwerke A.-G.], based in Leipzig.\n25 July 1914: The brand is registered as a trademark by the Imperial Patent Office Berlin (No. 316613).\n1924: Polydor is established as an export label for [l=Polyphon Record] products to be marketed outside of Germany.\n1927: Establishment of [l=Société Phonographique Française Polydor S.A.] in Paris, France, by the Danish and Swedish subsidiaries of Polyphonwerke A.-G.\n1932: Polyphonwerke A.-G. takes over the liquidated [l=Deutsche Grammophon-Aktiengesellschaft] and continues under this name.\n1941: After the takeover of Deutsche Grammophon by Siemens & Halske, the shellacs temporarily also appear under the label [l=Siemens Polydor].\n1946: Post-war production resumes and Polydor becomes the popular music arm. Classical music is separated to [l=Deutsche Grammophon].\n1949: Introduction of the red logo with golden stars and golden Polydor.\n1953: Introduction of the orange logo with golden stars and Polydor within a white background.\n1954: Establishment of [l=Polydor Records Ltd.] in the UK.\n1963: Introduction of the red logo with half-black disc and white Polydor, still in use today with slight alterations.\n1969: Polydor enters the US market.\n1972: Philips and Deutsche Grammophon form [l=PolyGram] and Polydor becomes part of [l=Polydor International].\n1991: Polydor becomes part of the [l=PolyGram Label Group].\n1998: Seagram acquires PolyGram and merges it into [l=Universal Music Group], effective January 1999.\n2003: The German division is merged with Island Records Germany to [l=Polydor Island Group].\n2005: The Polydor Island Group is merged into [l=Universal Music Domestic Division].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:1610","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":38404,"name":"Universal Music Group"},"sublabels":[{"id":1590866,"name":"10 Jahre Schlagerparade"},{"id":1426016,"name":"1000 Stimmen Singen Für Millionen"},{"id":2217868,"name":"14 Internationale Hits"},{"id":448191,"name":"2 Disques"},{"id":501263,"name":"2 For The Price Of 1"},{"id":297910,"name":"2 Super Oldies"},{"id":749630,"name":"2LP"},{"id":721806,"name":"99 (2)"},{"id":538734,"name":"ABBA Original Collection"},{"id":506905,"name":"ABC Paramount Série"},{"id":1189552,"name":"Anchor Point Records"},{"id":219390,"name":"Atlas Recordings (2)"},{"id":151563,"name":"Aura Records (3)"},{"id":52643,"name":"B&M Music"},{"id":1107342,"name":"Bert Kaempfert Collection"},{"id":232598,"name":"BGO Records (2)"},{"id":5490,"name":"Boiling Point"},{"id":106461,"name":"Bonanza"},{"id":1219604,"name":"Børnenes Ønskeplade"},{"id":1594159,"name":"Brüder Grimm"},{"id":729712,"name":"Budget Line Series"},{"id":198272,"name":"Caribbean Island"},{"id":6452,"name":"Carpet Records"},{"id":3560179,"name":"Carrera De Exitos"},{"id":525378,"name":"Chancellor Series"},{"id":2088235,"name":"Chanson ★★★★"},{"id":2941328,"name":"Chansons (5)"},{"id":83807,"name":"Cheese Music"},{"id":139946,"name":"Circle Of Sound"}